        "question": {
            "id": 18,
            "category": "debugging",
            "intent": "checkpoint_deserialization",
            "difficulty": "very_hard",
            "question": "A LangGraph application uses persistent checkpoints and is deployed across application versions. After a deployment, an existing workflow resumes successfully from a checkpoint, but some state values that were previously custom Python objects are now represented as plain dictionaries. The workflow does not immediately fail, but later behavior becomes incorrect because code expects the original object type. Review the persistence, checkpointing, backward-compatibility, and serializer implementation and determine whether this behavior is caused by checkpoint restoration, serializer compatibility, or graph execution. Explain: 1. How LangGraph serializes state into checkpoints and restores it during execution. 2. What happens when checkpoint data contains a type that the current runtime cannot deserialize or no longer recognizes. 3. Whether deserialization necessarily fails with an exception or can produce a fallback representation such as a dictionary. 4. How changes to application-defined/custom types across deployments can affect previously persisted checkpoints. 5. Why a workflow can resume successfully while still having semantically corrupted or incomplete state. 6. What role the checkpoint serializer and its type-registration/import mechanisms play in this behavior. 7. How backward compatibility should be considered when deploying a new application version against existing checkpoints. 8. Recommend a safe design or deployment strategy for handling persisted state whose Python types may change between versions.\n\nTo improve retrieval, investigate the question through these related search queries as well:\n- How does LangGraph serialize and deserialize checkpoint state?\n- How does JsonPlusSerializer handle unknown or unavailable Python types during checkpoint deserialization?\n- What happens when a custom Python type cannot be imported or reconstructed from persisted checkpoint data?\n- How does LangGraph handle backward compatibility for previously persisted checkpoints after an application deployment?\n- How do checkpoint serializer type registration and import mechanisms affect restoration of custom state objects?\n- Can LangGraph checkpoint deserialization fall back to a dictionary when the original type is unavailable?\n- How can incompatible custom state types cause a workflow to resume with corrupted or incomplete state?",
